{
  "schemaVersion": "newsstand-source-evidence-v1",
  "candidateId": "wiser-records-20260910",
  "reviewedThrough": "2026-09-10",
  "records": [
    {
      "id": "cms-wiser-current",
      "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/wiser",
      "publisher": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services",
      "authority": "Current primary program description for WISeR scope, review mechanism, safeguards, participants and payment design.",
      "accessObservation": "Direct HTTPS retrieval returned HTTP 200 on September 10, 2026.",
      "localEvidence": {
        "html": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/cms-wiser-20260910.html",
          "sha256": "584ae1a6189b2292e8a3c97451969f10785a8c07206a2f8ced2ac3038612c2a2"
        },
        "text": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/cms-wiser-20260910.txt",
          "sha256": "6c300b8fe216db3f99633d06a671f5eb63641c1a259d72b29bb9ce34f2f6662b"
        }
      },
      "passages": [
        "The WISeR Model helps protect American taxpayers by leveraging enhanced technologies, such as Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML), along with human clinical review, to ensure timely and appropriate Medicare payment for select items and services.",
        "WISeR will run for six performance years from January 1, 2026 to December 31, 2031 in six states: New Jersey, Ohio, Oklahoma, Texas, Arizona, and Washington.",
        "WISeR does not change Medicare coverage or payment policy.",
        "The model excludes inpatient-only services, emergency services, and services that would pose a substantial risk to patients if delayed.",
        "All recommendations for non-payment are determined by appropriately licensed clinicians who will apply standardized, transparent and evidence-based procedures to their review.",
        "WISeR does not apply to people with Medicare Advantage and will have no impact on them.",
        "Providers and suppliers for people with Original Medicare in selected regions will have the choice of submitting a prior authorization request for the model’s selected items and services or going through a post-service/pre-payment review.",
        "Model participants receive a percentage of the expenditures associated with averted wasteful, inappropriate care as a result of their reviews. That percentage is adjusted based on the participant’s performance on measures related to the process, including provider experience."
      ],
      "limitation": "This is CMS's current description of its own model and safeguards. It does not answer each problem in the released records or independently establish how well every vendor followed the requirements."
    },
    {
      "id": "cms-records-release",
      "url": "https://www.eff.org/files/2026/09/07/combined_records_-_2nd_interim_release.pdf",
      "publisher": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services records released through EFF litigation",
      "authority": "Primary documents for the payment design, audit safeguard, pre-launch vendor warning and dated operational tables cited in the article.",
      "accessObservation": "The 443-page PDF reopened by direct HTTPS retrieval on September 10, 2026 with HTTP 200; SHA-256 matched the supplied source identity. The seven cited pages were separately preserved for review.",
      "localEvidence": {
        "fullDownload": {
          "path": "/private/tmp/wiser-records.pdf",
          "sha256": "61b3076d2528c8542eea0bf18f740e8640a000dd72ddcfa91cd081ad772de56a",
          "pages": 443,
          "repositoryCopy": false
        },
        "citedPages": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/combined-records-cited-pages.pdf",
          "sha256": "84e84acabb1756956850e3b57b9ff7ef43e3c1a82cac13abc0168b21b96f7717",
          "originalPageNumbers": [22, 99, 216, 217, 234, 322, 410]
        }
      },
      "passages": [
        "Page 322, Table 2 lists 20,397 year-to-date decisions received for Virtix and Zyter through the March 30, 2026 report: 6,096 for Virtix and 14,301 for Zyter.",
        "Page 322, Table 3 splits those 20,397 decisions into 14,453 affirmations and 5,944 non-affirmations. Virtix has 2,863 affirmations and 3,233 non-affirmations; Zyter has 11,590 affirmations and 2,711 non-affirmations.",
        "Page 22 says payment savings are calculated from non-affirmations not followed by an affirmed resubmission or a successfully appealed claim denial. It also says one series of non-affirmations for the same beneficiary, item or service, and provider within 120 days earns only one participant payment.",
        "Page 234 says that as of March 30 Genzeon had 123 prior-authorization requests aged three days or older, with the oldest 83 days. It separates them into 14 awaiting a model-participant response, 44 awaiting a decision letter and 65 awaiting correction for creation of a tracking number or dismissal. The page does not say which stage applied to the oldest request.",
        "In a December 12, 2025 letter on pages 216 and 217, Innovaccer said it would meet the January 5, 2026 go-live date without full functionality. It cited changing requirements, new integration expectations, unclear governance and insufficient end-to-end testing time, and planned to auto-affirm requests while completing a phased rollout.",
        "Page 217 says Innovaccer expected the initial auto-affirmation period to last approximately 45 to 60 days and described the approach as a way to avoid a backlog of unprocessed requests while it finished, validated and deployed the full rules-based solution.",
        "Page 99 sets quality multipliers at 100 percent for aggregate scores of 85 to 100 percent, 95 percent for scores of 60 to 84 percent and 90 percent below 60 percent. It also says CMS conducts quarterly audits of participant records for consistency with Original Medicare coverage criteria.",
        "Page 410 groups January requests for two vendors into one-to-five, six-to-ten and eleven-to-twenty-one-day buckets. Those buckets do not disclose an exact rate for responses inside or outside 72 hours."
      ],
      "limitation": "The review was bounded to seven cited pages plus direct checks of the complete file's identity and page count, not a claim to have substantively reviewed every one of 443 pages. Operational tables are dated snapshots and some are cumulative; they must not be added together. Non-affirmation is not synonymous with a final denial of care."
    },
    {
      "id": "eff-wiser-records",
      "url": "https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2026/09/new-records-reveal-problems-medicares-ai-prior-authorization-experiment",
      "publisher": "Electronic Frontier Foundation",
      "authority": "September 8 source-release announcement and advocacy interpretation of records obtained through EFF's FOIA litigation.",
      "accessObservation": "Direct HTTPS retrieval returned HTTP 200 on September 10, 2026.",
      "localEvidence": {
        "html": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/eff-wiser-records-20260910.html",
          "sha256": "79695e236ad7d1568dd62d68c9c5597534fda94c10ec0f6d50138d4fc8e7571a"
        },
        "text": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/eff-wiser-records-20260910.txt",
          "sha256": "6d3223d275e4752d937650e586088f680937587806b9b3302d5bbe59bb62ab4f"
        }
      },
      "passages": [
        "EFF published the article and linked records on September 8, 2026, describing them as contracts, internal status reports and provider complaints obtained from CMS through litigation.",
        "EFF writes: ‘Two companies alone denied over 20,000 prior authorization requests in the first 3 months of the program,’ citing page 322.",
        "EFF says CMS records show delays, technical failures, inappropriate denials and patient harm, and says further CMS production remains ongoing."
      ],
      "limitation": "EFF is an advocacy party in the litigation. Its claim of more than 20,000 denials is not supported by its cited page: page 322 reports 20,397 total decisions, including 14,453 affirmations and 5,944 non-affirmations. The candidate must use the primary table rather than repeat EFF's number."
    },
    {
      "id": "kff-wiser-background",
      "url": "https://kffhealthnews.org/medicare/medicare-ai-prior-authorization-wiser-delays-errors/",
      "publisher": "KFF Health News",
      "authority": "Independent June 23 reporting for patient/provider experience, CMS and vendor responses, and program background before the September records release.",
      "accessObservation": "Direct HTTPS retrieval returned HTTP 200 on September 10, 2026.",
      "localEvidence": {
        "html": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/kff-wiser-20260623.html",
          "sha256": "a79dc87c3fcb4fa65c3032a8b716050bf348db178002c44cf66d75cee0a29781"
        },
        "text": {
          "path": "operations/product-stewards/newsstand/candidates/wiser-records-20260910/source-snapshots/kff-wiser-20260623.txt",
          "sha256": "2b391d078a31c5b95ff1ba36b88e19f29fa33163cada2eb24bd7c75cb857bce2"
        }
      },
      "passages": [
        "KFF reported on June 23 that patients, doctors and other health professionals it interviewed described confusion, errors, long waits and stress during WISeR's rollout.",
        "KFF reported that an Oklahoma Medicare beneficiary made an extra two-and-a-half-hour drive after learning his regular procedure needed preapproval and declined a proposed third trip that he understood was for paperwork.",
        "Humata CEO Jeremy Friese told KFF that its system gives an ‘immediate yes’ in 88 percent of cases for which clinical data supports an approval.",
        "Zyter CEO Sundar Subramanian told KFF that the company continuously monitors operations and works with stakeholders to address questions and improve provider experience.",
        "CMS official Abe Sutton told KFF that the model aims to reduce inappropriate care without delaying appropriate care and that payment backlogs were being resolved.",
        "KFF reported clinicians' suspicion of AI-generated errors, while Humata's CEO said he had not heard about AI hallucinations."
      ],
      "limitation": "This is June background, not new September reporting. The reported experiences establish real concerns and attributed accounts, not that AI caused every delay, that every non-affirmation was wrong or that the same conditions continued unchanged through September."
    }
  ],
  "sourceBoundary": {
    "verified": "The September release, exact selected record pages, current CMS scope and safeguards, and June independent reporting were reopened on September 10.",
    "notVerified": "No claim is made that all 443 pages were substantively reviewed, that AI caused every delay, that every non-affirmation was a final denial, or that the model applies to Medicare Advantage.",
    "correction": "The candidate must report 20,397 total decisions, 14,453 affirmations and 5,944 non-affirmations; it must not repeat EFF's more-than-20,000-denials claim."
  }
}
